    <!--
      Typography tokens, generated from the FINAL Figma export by
      tools/fonts/gen-typography.mjs. Do not hand-edit.

      One style per unique family + weight + size + lineHeight the frozen design
      actually uses - 34 in total. Roles map onto these tokens; the tokens
      carry no role semantics themselves.

      Two things in here do nothing on their own, and both are applied by
      MyataTypography instead. This is measured, not assumed - see
      TypographyProbeTest, which runs on API 24 and API 36.

      android:lineHeight is the framework attribute from API 28 and minSdk is 24.
      On 24 it is not merely ignored, it is unreadable: the id belongs to the
      framework's resource table, so a style lookup finds nothing at all. That is
      what myataLineHeight is for - the app's own attribute, same value, resolves
      everywhere. android:lineHeight stays because from API 28 the framework acts
      on it by itself, which is worth having for any token applied by something
      other than MyataTypography.

      Each style therefore carries tools:ignore="NewApi". Lint is right that the
      attribute is API 28 and wrong that it is a problem: below 28 the framework
      does not read it, nothing throws, and the line height arrives from
      myataLineHeight instead. Suppressed per style rather than by a baseline file,
      so it stays attached to the thing it is about.

      android:includeFontPadding does nothing on any API level, 36 included. It is
      a TextView attribute and this is a TextAppearance, which carries a fixed list
      that does not include it. It is written here because it is part of what the
      token means - the frozen line heights are Figma's, and Figma has no font
      padding - and MyataTypography is what actually turns it off.
    -->

